WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.440
Federated averaging trains one model for many clients, but it can leave some of them behind.

00:00:07.440 --> 00:00:08.835
This is FedPCD:

00:00:08.835 --> 00:00:10.230
priority-constrained federated aggregation.

00:00:12.833 --> 00:00:17.813
In federated learning, many clients train one shared model without ever sharing their data.

00:00:18.233 --> 00:00:22.651
Each round, the server sends the current model to a small sample of clients.

00:00:22.651 --> 00:00:26.753
Each of them trains it locally, and sends back how its copy changed.

00:00:27.200 --> 00:00:33.566
The server averages these updates, weighted by how much data each client holds, and takes a step.

00:00:33.566 --> 00:00:34.690
This is FedAvg.

00:00:35.133 --> 00:00:37.726
In practice, clients hold very different data.

00:00:37.726 --> 00:00:44.023
The smaller the Dirichlet parameter alpha, the more each client's data is dominated by a few classes.

00:00:45.133 --> 00:00:48.693
With data this different, client updates pull in different directions.

00:00:49.133 --> 00:00:50.919
Their average suits the majority.

00:00:50.919 --> 00:00:53.776
Measure how far each client gets along it:

00:00:53.776 --> 00:00:56.633
two clients gain, and the third one loses.

00:00:57.067 --> 00:01:01.745
Across a whole federation, this shows up as uneven accuracy.

00:01:01.745 --> 00:01:10.167
On CIFAR-10 with strong heterogeneity, FedAvg averages forty-eight percent, while its worst tenth of clients reaches barely nineteen.

00:01:10.600 --> 00:01:13.964
Fair federated learning asks for more than a good average.

00:01:13.964 --> 00:01:20.020
It wants a small spread across clients and a strong worst ten percent, without giving up global accuracy.

00:01:21.100 --> 00:01:25.358
Existing fairness methods change the aggregation in one of three ways.

00:01:25.358 --> 00:01:32.713
They reweight clients toward the worst off, they correct conflicting updates, or they search for a common descent direction.

00:01:33.163 --> 00:01:43.227
Here is what four of them do with the same three client updates.

00:01:43.677 --> 00:01:47.548
They differ in their details, but they share a pattern.

00:01:47.548 --> 00:01:52.968
They change the global direction, or they treat every client as a symmetric peer.

00:01:53.418 --> 00:01:57.676
Yet the average is already the update the shared objective prefers.

00:01:57.676 --> 00:02:02.321
Replacing it can be unnecessary when it already helps every client enough.

00:02:03.233 --> 00:02:11.663
FedPCD builds on Priority-Constrained Descent, or PCD, a method for problems where one objective matters more than the others.

00:02:12.100 --> 00:02:19.809
PCD follows the gradient of the primary objective, and bends away from it only as much as the secondary objectives require.

00:02:20.233 --> 00:02:26.723
FedPCD makes the federated average the primary direction, and turns every client into a secondary constraint.

00:02:27.167 --> 00:02:28.719
The average sets the course.

00:02:28.719 --> 00:02:32.757
The clients only decide when it has to bend, and by how much.

00:02:33.867 --> 00:02:36.511
Back to our three clients.

00:02:36.511 --> 00:02:39.156
FedPCD works with normalized directions:

00:02:39.156 --> 00:02:43.387
the average, g-tilde-zero, and one direction per client.

00:02:43.833 --> 00:02:46.722
Each client asks for a minimum of progress:

00:02:46.722 --> 00:02:51.416
the update must move at least a fraction tau along that client's direction.

00:02:51.416 --> 00:02:53.943
The directions that do form a half-space.

00:02:54.367 --> 00:02:58.417
Directions inside every half-space satisfy all clients at once.

00:02:58.417 --> 00:03:01.567
The average falls outside client three's half-space.

00:03:02.000 --> 00:03:09.360
So FedPCD projects the average onto this feasible region, to the closest direction that satisfies every client.

00:03:09.800 --> 00:03:17.100
Only client three's constraint is active, so the update is the average plus a small multiple of client three's direction.

00:03:17.100 --> 00:03:20.750
The other two clients still get more than their minimum.

00:03:21.167 --> 00:03:23.811
Formally, this is a small quadratic program.

00:03:23.811 --> 00:03:29.477
Stay as close as possible to the average, subject to one progress constraint per client.

00:03:30.567 --> 00:03:35.507
When the average already satisfies every client, no constraint is active.

00:03:35.507 --> 00:03:40.897
FedPCD then returns the average unchanged, and the update is exactly FedAvg.

00:03:41.333 --> 00:03:48.043
When a client falls short, only its constraint switches on, and FedPCD adds just enough of that client's direction.

00:03:48.467 --> 00:03:51.255
The tolerance tau sets the minimum progress.

00:03:51.255 --> 00:03:56.434
At zero, FedPCD only asks that no client gets worse, to first order.

00:03:56.434 --> 00:04:03.207
As tau grows, the constraints tighten, and the update bends further toward the clients that need it.

00:04:04.300 --> 00:04:08.385
In practice, only five or ten percent of clients join each round.

00:04:08.385 --> 00:04:11.790
So each round's program sees only a handful of clients.

00:04:12.233 --> 00:04:18.063
A client with unusual data can go unseen for many rounds, and while it is away, nothing protects it.

00:04:18.500 --> 00:04:21.122
FedPCD gives the server a short memory.

00:04:21.122 --> 00:04:27.490
It keeps each client's most recent direction for H rounds, and every cached direction adds a constraint.

00:04:27.933 --> 00:04:31.398
The primary direction still comes only from the current round.

00:04:31.398 --> 00:04:34.863
History changes who is protected, not what the average is.

00:04:35.300 --> 00:04:43.770
With a hundred clients, ten percent participation and a ten-round window, about sixty-nine clients are represented in expectation, instead of ten.

00:04:44.867 --> 00:04:47.261
Here is the paper's small simulation:

00:04:47.261 --> 00:04:53.647
thirty clients, three per round, and a minority of five whose directions conflict with everyone else's.

00:04:54.067 --> 00:04:57.629
Without history, the program never holds more than three constraints.

00:04:57.629 --> 00:05:01.547
With history, the count climbs toward twenty as the cache fills.

00:05:01.967 --> 00:05:05.590
A minority client first enters the cache in round three.

00:05:05.590 --> 00:05:11.387
In round sixteen, no minority client is sampled, but a cached one still constrains the update.

00:05:11.833 --> 00:05:14.782
With history, FedPCD keeps protecting the minority.

00:05:14.782 --> 00:05:18.993
Without it, the update follows whoever happened to be sampled.

00:05:20.100 --> 00:05:21.738
Why does this converge?

00:05:21.738 --> 00:05:23.375
Start with a trap.

00:05:23.375 --> 00:05:29.925
Client gradients can cancel, so their average shrinks to zero while no single client is done.

00:05:30.375 --> 00:05:34.054
FedPCD's constraints keep the update aligned with every client.

00:05:34.054 --> 00:05:40.595
Under a uniform alignment condition, the update cannot vanish while the clients' own gradients remain large.

00:05:41.045 --> 00:05:51.055
With full participation, exact local gradients and no history, the average squared norm of the global gradient then falls as one over T, with constant learning rates.

00:05:51.505 --> 00:05:55.228
The same rate holds for the clients' own gradients.

00:05:55.228 --> 00:05:59.365
So, under these conditions, every limit point is composite multi-stationary:

00:05:59.365 --> 00:06:02.261
each client is stationary on its own.

00:06:02.261 --> 00:06:07.225
That is stronger than Pareto stationarity, where nonzero gradients may simply cancel.

00:06:08.300 --> 00:06:12.225
Put together, a FedPCD round looks like FedAvg on the clients.

00:06:12.225 --> 00:06:15.080
They train locally and send back their updates.

00:06:15.500 --> 00:06:22.510
The server turns these into pseudo-gradients, refreshes its cache, normalizes every direction, and solves the small program.

00:06:22.960 --> 00:06:28.607
It rescales the result to the length of the average update, and takes the step.

00:06:28.607 --> 00:06:31.996
When no constraint is active, this is exactly FedAvg.

00:06:33.033 --> 00:06:34.566
Now, the experiments.

00:06:34.566 --> 00:06:45.293
We test on CIFAR-10, CIFAR-100 and Fashion-MNIST, with a hundred clients, five or ten percent participation, and three levels of heterogeneity.

00:06:45.293 --> 00:06:51.423
That makes eighteen settings, each averaged over five seeds, against ten baselines.

00:06:51.858 --> 00:06:59.768
FedPCD has the highest global accuracy in all eighteen settings, and the highest worst-ten-percent accuracy in seventeen.

00:07:00.192 --> 00:07:07.012
On CIFAR-10, the margin is largest where the data is most heterogeneous and participation lowest.

00:07:07.012 --> 00:07:15.652
There, FedPCD reaches seventy-one point nine percent, against sixty-five point six for FedLF, and forty-eight point one for FedAvg.

00:07:16.092 --> 00:07:22.072
Its worst ten percent of clients reach fifty-two point nine percent, more than fourteen points above FedLF.

00:07:22.492 --> 00:07:32.105
On CIFAR-100, FedPCD leads the strongest competitor, CRAFT, by between one point seven and five point six points of global accuracy.

00:07:32.105 --> 00:07:43.092
On Fashion-MNIST, where the best methods already pass eighty-five percent, margins are smaller, and in one setting FedLF keeps the better worst ten percent.

00:07:44.167 --> 00:07:47.402
Do these gains reach the clients who need them?

00:07:47.402 --> 00:07:51.357
Here is every client's accuracy on CIFAR-10, pooled over five seeds.

00:07:51.807 --> 00:07:58.437
Compared with FedAvg, FedPCD shifts the whole distribution to the right, and its lower tail thins out.

00:07:58.873 --> 00:08:06.304
Against the strongest baseline, FedLF, more of FedPCD's clients sit near the top.

00:08:06.754 --> 00:08:12.979
Spread alone can mislead, because a method can look even by holding everyone down.

00:08:12.979 --> 00:08:21.429
On CIFAR-100, AFL has the lowest spread in all six settings, at global accuracies between five and fifteen percent.

00:08:21.872 --> 00:08:26.943
So we plot accuracy against spread, where the bottom right is best.

00:08:26.943 --> 00:08:36.662
On CIFAR-10 and Fashion-MNIST at five percent participation, FedPCD is at least as accurate and at least as even as all ten baselines.

00:08:37.089 --> 00:08:45.693
On CIFAR-100, FedPCD's spread is slightly higher, because spread grows with the accuracy it is measured around.

00:08:45.693 --> 00:08:52.779
Jain's fairness index, which does not, ranks FedPCD first in all six CIFAR-100 settings.

00:08:53.733 --> 00:08:56.015
FedPCD also gets there sooner.

00:08:56.015 --> 00:09:06.053
On CIFAR-10 at five percent participation, it reaches FedAvg's final accuracy by round one hundred ten, four and a half times sooner.

00:09:06.500 --> 00:09:12.670
It passes the final accuracy of the strongest baseline, FedLF, by round two hundred eighty.

00:09:13.100 --> 00:09:25.470
Across all eighteen settings, FedPCD reaches FedAvg's final accuracy within ten to forty-four percent of the rounds, and passes the strongest baseline's final accuracy early in sixteen of them.

00:09:26.567 --> 00:09:28.368
What makes this work?

00:09:28.368 --> 00:09:30.169
Above all, the history.

00:09:30.169 --> 00:09:40.077
On CIFAR-10 at ten percent participation, removing it drops FedPCD from seventy-four point eight percent to fifty-nine point three, close to FedAvg.

00:09:40.500 --> 00:09:42.385
The other parts help too.

00:09:42.385 --> 00:09:50.300
Removing the primary direction costs about four points, removing normalization about two, and setting tau to zero about half a point.

00:09:50.722 --> 00:09:56.639
Grow the history window, and accuracy climbs with the number of active constraints.

00:09:56.639 --> 00:10:04.376
At five percent participation, global accuracy rises from forty-eight percent without history to seventy-two with ten rounds.

00:10:04.809 --> 00:10:09.729
Most of the gain arrives within the first three rounds of memory, and the returns diminish after that.

00:10:10.800 --> 00:10:14.754
The average should lead, but not at any client's expense.

00:10:15.200 --> 00:10:24.720
FedPCD keeps the FedAvg direction, protects every recently seen client with a minimum-progress constraint, and bends only as much as those constraints require.

00:10:25.167 --> 00:10:30.567
Open directions include adaptive tolerances, and variants compatible with secure aggregation.
